==Galician==

===Etymology===
Attested since the 12th century in Medieval Latin documents. Either from {{der|gl|gem-pro|*kannǭ|t=can, tankard, mug, cup}}, or from {{inh|gl|la|canna|t=canne}}, and a suffix {{m|gl|-ada}}. Cognate with {{cog|en|can}}, {{cog|de|Kanne}}.<ref>{{R:es:DCECH|cañada}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|gl|/kaˈnaða̝/}}

===Noun===
{{gl-noun|f}}

# {{lb|gl|dated}} A former [[liquid measure]], usually of four ''[[cuartillo]]s'', especially used for [[wine]] and [[milk]]
#* {{quote-book|gl|year=1390|editor=Mª Luz Méndez Fernández|title=Contribución ó estudio dun libro das Tenzas da Catedral de Santiago|page=39
|passage=''dúas cabaaças de vjño de senllas medias '''canadas''' et quatro capões''
|translation=two gourds of wine, each one containing half a '''canada''', and four capons}}
# a wooden or tin cylindrical [[jug]], with [[spout]] and [[handle]]
#* {{quote-book|gl|year=1373|editor=Enrique Cal Pardo|title=Colección diplomática medieval do arquivo da catedral de Mondoñedo|location=Santiago|publisher=Consello da Cultura Galega|page=191
|passage=''dous picheles dstanno grandes et dous pequenos et duas '''canadas''' grandes''
|translation=two large tin tankards and two small ones and two large '''jugs'''}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|gl|canado}}
* {{l|gl|caneca}}

